  {
    "episode_id": 86732,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86732.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Coming up, he helped lead some of the most important and dangerous events of the civil rights movement, and was the youngest speaker at the March on Washington in 1963. I talk with Representative John Lewis about how he's still trying to keep Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.' 's dream alive. That's next on Dialog, stay tuned. Hello and welcome to Dialog, I'm Marcia Franklin. Some people are present when history is made, others make history. My guest today is one such person. Representative John Lewis was a poor child in Alabama when he heard the words of Reverend Martin Luther King Jr. on the radio. He decided right then that he felt compelled to both meet the man and change his own life. Within just a few years, he had achieved both goals and found himself the youngest member of Reverend King's Inner Circle. Lewis would go on to help organize some of the most seminal activities of the African-American civil rights movement, including the Freedom Rides in Mississippi and the March from Selma to Montgomery. Along the way, he would be seriously injured in beatings by state troopers and white mobs. Eventually, Lewis became a U.S. representative from Georgia, a post he's held for nearly 30 years. He's now the only surviving member of the so-called big six leaders of the civil rights movement and the recipient of our country's highest civilian honor, the Medal of Freedom. The author of two books about his life, he's recently ventured into new literary territory with a comic book trilogy about the civil rights movement called March. that he's riding with one of his staffers. Now, Representative Lewis was at the Sun Valley Writers Conference in 2014 to talk about his life and his new book. I spoke with him there about his legacy and the challenges for the next generation of civil rights leaders. Thanks so much for being here. Welcome to Idaho.",
