    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "was not a supporter of the idea of a march in Washington. He raised the question and said, won't that be violence and chaos and disordered? You would never get us some rights bill through the Congress. And it was Mr. Randolph, A. Philip Randolph, the dean of Black leadership, who spoke up and said in his baritone voice, Mr. President, this will be an orderly, peaceful, nonviolent protest. And we left that meeting with President Kennedy. I remember so well, and we said we had a meaningful and productive meeting with the president. We told him we were going to march on Washington. And at the first meeting, we invited four major white religious and labor leaders to join us in issuing the call for the march. So we're 10 of us that spoke. And the march went so well. I spoke number six, Dr. King spoke number 10. And when the march was all over, President Kennedy invaded us back down to the White House, and he stood in the door to over office. He was beaming like a proud father. He kept saying, you did a good job, you did a good job. And when he got to Dr. King, he said, you did a good job, and you had a dream.",
