    "turn_text": "March, this book, book one, book two, book three, would be, I believe, a strong instrument. a powerful tool to set to young people, and to those not so young, that may not have been old enough, not old enough to remember what happened and how it happened, that the methods and philosophy that guided us to bring about the changes we have in America today. If someone had told me back in 1963, in 1965, then the March in Washington, or the March from Selma to Montgomery, that we would be where we are today. That probably was so you crazy. You out of your mind, you don't know what you're talking about. If someone had told me that I would live to see a man, a color in the White House, I don't know what I would have said, but I would have been in disbelief. So when President Barack Obama was elected, President, that might, you know, I cried. And some members of the press asked me, why are you crying so much? I said, there's tears of happiness. There's tears of joy. So what are you going to do when you're inaugurated? I said, if I have some tears left, I'm going to cry some more. That's exactly what I did.",
