  },
  {
    "episode_id": 86732,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86732.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 55,
    "turn_text": "But during the 60s, the national level, we had people coming together. It really didn't matter whether you were a Democrat, a Republican, or an independent. If you visit my office in Washington, you see, You will see on the morning of the March of Washington, the ten of us, including Martin Luther King, Jr., standing together with Republican members of the Senate and the House, working together to get the Civil Rights Act of 1964 passed, and later working together to get the Voting Rights Act passed. Is it possible for people to lay down the burden of differences?",
