    "turn_idx": 65,
    "turn_text": "Well, in May 1961, during the Freedom Ride, when I was 21 years old, had all of my hair and a few pounds lighter, we attempted to desegregate a waiting room. at a Greyhound bus station and a group of young men attacked us and left us lying in a pool of blood. Many years later, more than 50 years later, one of the guys who had been a member of the Klan came to my office in Washington with his son. His son had been encouraging his father to seek out the people that he had ruled. And this man came to my office and said, Mr. Lewis, and one of the people who beat you and left you bloody. I want to apologize. Will you forgive me? Your son started crying. He started crying. I started crying. They hugged me. I hugged them back. and we called each other brothers. And I saw this man through other time. But I've had people in my native state of Alabama, in Georgia, woke up to me, and said, I want to apologize to you for what we did. One young man came up and said, I want to apologize to you. They have all of the white people of Alabama. I've had police chiefs who One in particular met me at a church meeting in Montgomery and said, when you came in Montgomery, our police department stood away and it allowed a mob to beat you. So we have a better police department today. They must know something about non-violence, something about Martin Luther King, Jr., about Rosa Park, about Selma, about what happened in Montgomery, what happened in Birmingham. You've been on to a set because of what you did and the admiration I have for you. You said, I'm going to take off my badge. I'm going to give you my bad. And I said, chief, you can't do that. You're the chief of police. You need your badge. He said, Congressman, I can get another one.",
