    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 86732,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86732.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 101,
    "turn_text": "As I said, my mother raised me. And when you see in the, even the 80s, the way they treated a single mother. I'll never forget going to, I think it was a property tax commission, and they raised her property taxes, and she had this little teeny house, and it was all we had. My mother goes there to appeal it, because she couldn't afford it. She's working a job, she's got her son in public school, she's trying to do it, and they just totally blew her off, ignored her, and asked, what are you doing here, little lady, in all this sort of denigrating language? And I was maybe five or six years old and I just started shouting, that's not right. That's not fair. Only coming to realize that that's the same motto that Congressman has lived his life by so much. So I was exposed to this at a very early age. It's amazing when we think of civil rights issues. This is something that encompasses income, it encompasses gender, sexual orientation. And so I was sensitized to them at a very, very early age, and it stayed with me my whole life. When I went to college, I was an activist, or as some call me, a rabble rouser. I worked on a clean car issue because child asthma is the number one reason children miss school. And so if you want children to do better and have a better education, you first need them to go to school. While I was in Connecticut and this is a place that's very dependent on cars and the air is very poor. So if you want to fix that child asthma rate, you have to start by making the air cleaner and you start by making the cars cleaner. And so we passed that bill and I was the 19-year-old campaign manager for that. And so these have always been issues that in various forms have been very important to me. And so to work with the congressman, it gives you such a broader range of opportunities to explore correcting injustice, writing wrongs. Every day we go to work in the congressional office is another opportunity to make the world just a little bit better or to stop in some cases, all too often a slide backwards.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
